Step 1: To find the remainder, we need to use synthetic division. We'll start by setting up the synthetic division table.

## Step 2: List the roots of the divisor and write a line to separate the coefficients of the terms of the polynomial and the remainders.
We can start the process now.
 \\[
 \begin{array}{c|cccc}
  3 &&  & 1 &  & 0 &  & 1 & & 2\\
 \end{array}
]

Step 3: Bring down the first coefficient to the bottom row of the synthetic division table.
The first number is 1.
 \\[
 \begin{array}{c|cccc}
  3 &&  & 1 &  & 0 &  & 1 & & 2\\
   &   & \downarrow & &  &  &  & &\\
 \hline
  3  && 1 & & &  & & & \\
 \end{array}
]

## Step 4: Multiply the number at the bottom of the left column by the root and write the result in the top row.
We multiply 3 and 1 to get 3.
 \\[
 \begin{array}{c|cccc}
  3 &&  & 1 &  & 0 &  & 1 & & 2\\
   &   & \downarrow & &  &  &  & &\\
 \hline 3 && & 1 & & 0 & & 3 & & \\
 \end{array}
]

## Step 5: Add the terms in the top row and write the result on the bottom row.
We add 0 and 3 to get 3.
 \\[
 \begin{array}{c|cccc}
  3 & &  &1 & & 0 & & 1 & & 2\\
   &   & \downarrow & &  &  &  & &\\
 \hline  3 &&  & 1 & & 3 & & &  \\
 \end{array}
]

## Step 6: Multiply the number at the bottom of the left column by the root and write the result in the top row.
We multiply 3 and 3 to get 9.
 \\[
 \begin{array}{c|cccc}
  3 &&  &1 & & 0 & & 1 & & 2\\
   &   & \downarrow & &  &  &  & &\\
 \hline 3 && & 1 & & 3 & & &  \\
   & & \uparrow  & & &  & & & 9 & \\
 \end{array}
]

## Step 7: Add the terms in the top row and write the result on the bottom row.
We add 9 and 1 to get 10.
 \\[
 \begin{array}{c|cccc}
   3 & &  & 1 & & 0 & & 1 & & 2\\
    &   & \downarrow & &  &  &  & &\\
    \hline
      & & 3 & & 10 & &  & & \\
 \end{array}
]

## Step 8: Multiply the number at the bottom of the left column by the root and write the result in the top row.
We multiply 3 and 10 to get 30.
 \\[
 \begin{array}{c|cccc}
  3 &&  & 1 & & 0 & & 1 & & 2\\
   &   & \downarrow & &  &  &  & &\\
 \hline  &  & & 3 & & 10 & & & \\
   & & \uparrow & & & & & & 30 & \\
 \end{array}
]

## Step 9: Add the terms in the top row and write the result on the bottom row.
We add 30 and 2 to get 32.
 \\[
 \begin{array}{c|cccc}
   3 &&  & 1 & & 0 & & 1 & & 2\\
    &   & \downarrow & &  &  &  & &\\
    \hline
     & & 3 &&  & 32 & &  \\
 \end{array}
]
