(\sqrt{2+\sqrt x})^2 & = 3^2 \\
2+\sqrt{x} & = 9
\end{align*}

## Step 2: Isolate the radical.
To isolate $\sqrt{x}$, we need to subtract $2$ from both sides of the equation. This gives us:

\begin{align*}
2 + \sqrt{x} - 2 & = 9 - 2 \\
\sqrt{x} & = 7
\end{align*}

## Step 3: Square both sides of the equation again.
To solve for $x$, we square both sides of this equation. This gives us:

\begin{align*}
\sqrt{x}^2 & = 7^2 \\
x & = 49
\end{align*}

